---
import SiteLayout from '../../components/SiteLayout.astro';
import ProductArchitecture from '../../components/ProductArchitecture.astro';
import ProductJourney from '../../components/ProductJourney.astro';
---
<SiteLayout title="About Ember — a clearer Monero mining manager" description="What Ember is building for Windows Monero mining, how its current foundation works, and what remains in development.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-intro product-intro"><div><span class="page-kicker">EMBER / THE PRODUCT</span><h1>More of the mining process, made understandable.</h1><p>Ember is a Windows desktop project that helps people configure and manage Monero CPU mining without hiding the important choices.</p></div><p class="release-note"><strong>IN DEVELOPMENT · NO PUBLIC DOWNLOAD</strong><br />A controlled owner-machine Start → Mining session has been observed. Stop/Quit acceptance and release hardening remain open.</p></header>
    <section class="product-band" aria-labelledby="product-purpose"><div><div class="eyebrow">Why Ember exists</div><h2 id="product-purpose">A mining engine shouldn't have to be the whole experience.</h2><p>XMRig provides the mining engine. Ember's role is to make setup, consent and local process state easier to follow, while preserving the technical detail when it matters.</p></div><div class="feature-rows"><div class="feature-row"><span>01</span><div><strong>Configure with context</strong><p>Enter a public receiving address and pool details, then review the choices and disclosures before starting.</p></div></div><div class="feature-row"><span>02</span><div><strong>Keep a visible boundary</strong><p>Ember prepares and supervises its own XMRig process; the selected pool remains an external service.</p></div></div><div class="feature-row"><span>03</span><div><strong>Understand the profile</strong><p>Quiet, Balanced and Performance set configured thread counts; they are not wattage or temperature controls.</p></div></div></div></section>
    <section class="section product-journey-section" aria-labelledby="journey-title"><div class="section-heading"><div class="eyebrow">THE PRODUCT JOURNEY</div><h2 id="journey-title">From considered setup to observed work.</h2><p>Ember adds a reviewable path around XMRig: it keeps configuration, consent, process state and the miner’s reported signals in one local experience.</p></div><ProductJourney /></section>
    <section class="section product-architecture-section" id="how-it-works" aria-label="How the user, Ember, XMRig and mining pool relate"><ProductArchitecture /></section>
    <p class="product-resource-note">The site's <a href="/learn/">concept guides</a>, <a href="/troubleshoot/">troubleshooting resources</a> and <a href="/tools/">local tools</a> explain the same decisions and signals from the reader's point of view.</p>
    <section class="product-band" aria-labelledby="product-controls"><div><div class="eyebrow">WHAT STAYS VISIBLE</div><h2 id="product-controls">Choices before activity. State during a session.</h2><p>Start is explicit and readiness is not treated as a mining result. During a session, Ember presents the signals XMRig reports and identifies when a reading is delayed or unavailable.</p></div><div class="product-status"><article class="status-tile"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> IMPLEMENTED</span><h3>Guided setup</h3><p>Wallet, manual pool, configured threads and disclosures are reviewed before an explicit start.</p></article><article class="status-tile"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> OWNER-TESTED</span><h3>Start → Mining</h3><p>A current-build owner session reached authenticated XMRig telemetry, a connected pool and real hashrate.</p></article><article class="status-tile is-open"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> ACCEPTANCE OPEN</span><h3>Stop / Quit</h3><p>Process cleanup is implemented; owner acceptance of Stop and Quit on the current release remains open.</p></article><article class="status-tile is-open"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> NOT RELEASED</span><h3>Public distribution</h3><p>Legal review, clean-machine checks and release hardening remain open. No public download is available.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="cta-panel"><div class="eyebrow">A note on availability</div><h2>Ember is being built, not offered as a public download.</h2><p>Read the implementation and release caveats before forming a view of what the product currently supports.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/trust/">Trust and transparency <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
